.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_full \- declare and create a hash map with custom allocation, hashing and key equality
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_full(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                     custom_hashing_proc, custom_key_equality_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates the map. It is the widest of the construct
macros: chmap_construct(3) is this macro with the default allocator and
the map's own hashing, chmap_construct_mp(3) gives only an allocator,
and chmap_construct_ch(3) gives only a hashing procedure.

Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails. When
the caller must handle a failure instead of the failure being fatal, use
chmap_init_full(3) on an already declared handle.

The map picks its implementation strategy from the key and value types, as
chmap_create_full(3) describes. A custom hashing procedure does not change
that choice, but a custom key equality procedure selects separate chaining.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                Key type
val_t                Value type
mmgmt_procs          Pointer to ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                     default allocator
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for the key type
custom_key_equality_proc
                     A ccol_key_equality_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's
                     own key comparison
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure receives the key bytes and their length, and returns
an unsigned long. It must return the same value for two keys that the map
considers equal; for a char * key, equal means equal contents, not an
equal pointer.

The return value does not need to avalanche, because the map finalizes
the value that a custom procedure returns with its own bit-mixing step
before it reduces the value to a slot or a bucket. A procedure that puts
all of its entropy in the low bits (a sum, a small identifier, a truncated
checksum) therefore still distributes correctly. This is why the contract
of the procedure is only "any function of the key that respects equality",
with no rule about which end of the result carries the entropy.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
custom_key_equality_proc receives both keys and both sizes, and returns
true when the two are the same key. The map uses it in place of its
built-in comparison. It must agree with custom_hashing_proc: two keys
that it reports as equal must get the same hash. A key equality procedure
without a hashing procedure makes the creation fail, and this macro then
stops the program. A struct key with padding bytes needs both
procedures; see chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares a variable and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the handle; use chmap_construct_full_scoped(3)
when scope exit must destroy it instead
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
unsigned long my_hash(const void *key, size_t len);

chmap_construct_full(cache, char *, int, &my_procs, my_hash, NULL);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(cache, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(cache);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_construct_mp (3),
.BR chmap_construct_ch (3),
.BR chmap_construct_full_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
